{"topic": "faculty", "review": {"readability_score": 4, "source_accuracy_score": 4, "qualification_retention_score": 3, "unsupported_claims": ["The reporting-period line lists both academic-year (2015-16...) and Fall periods as applying \"across these sources\". This implies uniform coverage across all HR topics, although the salary tables (T4, T6) use academic years and the others use Fall.", "\"Salaries and pay outlays ... derived average salaries\" is loosely supported. The derived HR table (T7) contains average salaries equated to 9 months for instructional staff only, which the phrase does not convey.", "\"Staff counts ... by occupational category\" omits that counts cover full- and part-time staff and include medical school status. The text reads as one generic staff-count measure."], "missing_qualifications": ["Instructional staff and new-hire tables apply to degree-granting institutions with 15 or more full-time employees. The candidate drops the degree-granting label.", "The salary outlay tables cover nonmedical staff only. The candidate omits \"nonmedical\", which is a population qualification.", "The candidate omits the T2 race/ethnicity and gender source-label caveat. Reporting was optional in even-numbered years before 2016, so even-year files from 2015 or earlier have incomplete institution coverage.", "Contract-category labels changed in 2016 (multi-year and indefinite contracts were previously combined), and new-hire definition windows changed. Both affect comparability across years, and the candidate says nothing about either.", "The retention measure's cohort is not tied to its outcome date, for example a fall 2023 entering cohort measured in fall 2024. Stating \"first-year retention\" with the Fall 2015-2024 range blurs this.", "The candidate mentions \"institutions with undergraduate students\" but omits that only academic-year institutions report entering class data and that short-program institutions lack retention data."], "comments": ["The prose is concise and readable. The required bounded-search and availability qualifications are retained. The merged groupings (T1+T2, T3+T5, T4+T6+T7) are acceptable as navigation but do not certify equivalence.", "The period line mixing Fall and academic-year labels without mapping them to topics is the main accuracy risk. The original template kept the ranges separate by topic.", "The \"Coverage varies by measure\" hedge partly offsets the period issue but does not name which tables differ."]}}
{"topic": "expenses", "status": "accepted", "seconds": 5.851275100023486, "usage": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "calls": 1, "input_tokens": 42379, "output_tokens": 718, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "estimated_api_usd": 0.091938, "pricing_basis": "Estimated standard Anthropic API rates checked 2026-10-20; excludes taxes/provider adjustments.", "usage_complete": true, "latency_seconds": 5.597}, "reason": null}
{"topic": "expenses", "review": {"readability_score": 4, "source_accuracy_score": 4, "qualification_retention_score": 3, "unsupported_claims": ["\"Separate indicator sets exist for different accounting standards\" is supported (GASB, FASB and for-profit), but the draft does not name them. The cited Finance example fields are GASB-only, so the \"each spending category's share\" wording could imply all institution types.", "\"Student cost of attendance estimates ... for academic-year and vocational programs\" merges two differently scoped tables. The academic-year table covers full-time, first-time undergraduates, while the vocational table covers the largest program, with tuition and books/supplies for up to five more. The draft does not say this.", "\"Browse cost of attendance, tuition and fees, food, housing and other expenses ... for undergraduate and graduate students\" implies cost of attendance exists for graduate students. The source gives cost of attendance for first-time undergraduates only. Graduate students have tuition and fees only, and doctoral-professional students are excluded from the graduate figures.", "The Cost-survey bullet adds no source-label caveat and presents Cost as a plain parallel to the IC tables. The IC bullet says \"room and board\" while the Cost bullet says \"food, housing\", which could suggest different measures.", "The branch campus bullet cites only the 2023-24 passage (R119923P9) but the topic spans 2021-22 to 2023-24. \"Some branch campus locations\" is reasonable, since only branches that enroll full-time, first-time undergraduates are covered.", "The libraries bullet states \"library expenses ... and total library spending per FTE\", which is supported by the table definition. However, the explicit expense-related fields are not shown, and the example fields in the template were all collection shares. The library coverage is only partly about expenses."], "missing_qualifications": ["The Academic Libraries reporting periods list \"2015-16; 2016-17; 2017-18; 2019-20\" plus fiscal years. The source label for 2018-19 is actually 2017-18, so the label change and the missing 2018-19 label are not flagged. The draft implies uniform period coverage with \"Coverage varies by measure\".", "Institutional Characteristics is stated as 2015-16 through 2023-24, but the branch campus data covers only 2021-22 to 2023-24. The draft dropped the per-topic period note.", "The draft drops the caveat that the matches are codebook and physical-table matches, not confirmation of reported values. It partly keeps this through the required qualification that availability does not guarantee reported values.", "The draft lacks any warning that definitions, populations and breakdowns differ by year. The generic \"definitions vary\" line only partly covers this.", "The Finance caveat that derived variables may include child-campus data allocated from the parent institution is not mentioned. It is optional.", "The Cost survey (2024-25) is provisional, and the Finance 2024 and Library 2024 tables are provisional releases. The draft does not say so."], "comments": ["The draft is readable and plain-language, and the technical identifiers were reasonably moved out of the prose. The required qualifications appear in full.", "The main weakness is that population and measure scoping was flattened, especially the graduate-student implication in the Cost bullet and the merging of IC tables with different scopes.", "The library bullet is grounded in the table description, but it is somewhat off the user's \"expenses\" question compared with the template. The draft does not mark which data are expense measures versus collection shares.", "Shorter wording is acceptable, but \"Coverage varies by measure\" does not replace the per-source period and label notes."]}}
