{"topic": "academic libraries", "review": {"readability_score": 4, "source_accuracy_score": 3, "qualification_retention_score": 3, "unsupported_claims": ["Instructional staff tables described as covering 'salary outlays' alongside rank, tenure, gender and race/ethnicity; only the SAL_IS table has salary outlays, and the S_SIS/S_IS tables are counts only.", "Merged HR reporting-period list ('2015-16; Fall 2015; ...') implies every HR measure covers all listed periods under both labelings.", "'Library staff in noninstructional counts' implies a library-specific measure, but the category also bundles academic affairs and other education services occupations.", "Cost: 'first-time undergraduates' drops the full-time and degree/certificate-seeking population.", "Student charges described only as 'Tuition, required fees and per-credit charges' without the full-time vs part-time and undergraduate vs graduate/professional distinctions."], "missing_qualifications": ["Academic Libraries source-label mismatches (2018-19 release labeled 2017-18; title/period inconsistencies) and the missing 2018-19 period.", "Library expenditure data apply only to degree-granting institutions with total expenditures over $100,000.", "HR instructional tables apply only to degree-granting institutions with 15 or more full-time employees.", "Race/ethnicity and gender HR reporting was optional in even-numbered years before 2016.", "Cost table applies only to institutions that charge by academic year.", "Mixed Fall vs academic-year period labels are not separated by table."], "comments": ["Readable and concise, and it correctly marks non-library items as not library-specific. The required bounded-search and coverage-varies qualifications are present.", "The main accuracy flaw is the combined staff-table sentence that attributes salary outlays to all three tables.", "Pooling the Human Resources periods into one interleaved list loses per-table period coverage and label differences that the template kept.", "Citations are present but do not support the salary-outlay claim for T6/T7."]}}
